---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import StatusBadge from '../components/StatusBadge.astro';
import { ALGORITHM_INFO, ALGORITHMS, STATUS_INFO, STATUSES, UPCOMING_ALGORITHMS } from '@pqc-deps/core';
import { SITE } from '../config';
---

<Base
  title="Methodology"
  description="How PQC Deps defines post-quantum readiness statuses, collects and verifies evidence, and keeps entries up to date."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Methodology</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      How we decide a library's status, where the evidence comes from, and how often it's checked.
    </p>

    <h2>Status definitions</h2>
    <p>Each library gets exactly one overall status.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Status</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          STATUSES.map((s) => (
            <tr>
              <td><StatusBadge status={s} /></td>
              <td>{STATUS_INFO[s].description}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      "Where it applies" matters. Each entry lists the algorithms that are relevant to it: a JWT
      library has no TLS key exchange, and a TLS library may not expose signatures. A library is{' '}
      <em>ready</em> when post-quantum protection is on by default for at least one applicable
      algorithm, in a stable release, without experimental flags.
    </p>

    <h2>Algorithms tracked</h2>
    <ul>
      {
        ALGORITHMS.map((a) => (
          <li>
            <strong>{ALGORITHM_INFO[a].label}</strong> ({ALGORITHM_INFO[a].standard}):{' '}
            {ALGORITHM_INFO[a].kind.toLowerCase()}
            {UPCOMING_ALGORITHMS.includes(a) && '. Tracked ahead of standardization.'}
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <p>
      For each algorithm we record whether it's supported (yes, partial, no or unknown), the first
      stable version, whether it's on by default or opt-in, and whether it's experimental.
    </p>

    <h2>Evidence rules</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Every claim cites a source</strong>: release notes, changelogs, documentation,
        commits, issues, pull requests or maintainer posts.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Every source carries a verbatim quote</strong>, so you can find the exact sentence on
        the page.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No source means unknown.</strong> We don't fill gaps from memory or assumptions.
      </li>
      <li>
        Each entry has a <em>last verified</em> date. Entries older than {SITE.staleAfterDays} days
        are flagged as possibly out of date.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How data is collected</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        An automated pipeline fetches each library's recent releases, changelog, documentation and
        GitHub issues and pull requests that mention ML-KEM, Kyber, ML-DSA, Dilithium, SLH-DSA,
        X25519MLKEM768, post-quantum or PQC.
      </li>
      <li>
        A language model drafts a status proposal from that material, citing a quote for every field.
      </li>
      <li>
        The pipeline checks that each quote really appears in the fetched page. Any field whose quote
        can't be found is dropped back to unknown.
      </li>
      <li>
        The proposal is opened as a pull request. <strong>A human reviews every change</strong> before
        it's published. The pipeline can't publish on its own.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Update frequency</h2>
    <p>
      The pipeline re-runs weekly to catch new releases. Anyone can suggest a correction at any time
      through a GitHub issue or pull request.
    </p>

    <h2>Delegation</h2>
    <p>
      Many packages don't implement cryptography themselves: they call the platform's crypto,
      OpenSSL, or another library. We record this as a <em>delegates to</em> link and show the chain,
      so you can see which library actually needs to support post-quantum algorithms.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      This is not a security audit. Support in a library doesn't mean your application uses it: you
      may still need to upgrade, configure or call new APIs. Always check the linked sources.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
